# ★ THE FIX IS ONE CHARACTER OF PARENTHESIS, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE LESSON.
#
# The widening cast was already there. It was applied to the SUM:
#
#     (uint64_t)(offset + length)      offset and length are both uint32_t, so
#                                      the addition happens in unsigned int and
#                                      WRAPS; the cast then widens a value that
#                                      has already lost the carry
#
#     ((uint64_t)offset + length)      one operand is widened FIRST, so the
#                                      addition happens in 64 bits and cannot
#                                      wrap
#
# A reviewer scanning for "is there a cast" finds one in both versions. That is
# why negative/test_offset_length.c asserts the behaviour instead of inspecting
# the text, and why its defect 1 is this expression rather than a paraphrase of
# it: same shape, same operand widths, same outcome.
#
# ★ The same expression at GET_CERTIFICATE's 16-bit field widths does NOT wrap,
# because uint16_t operands are promoted to int before the addition. The
# advisory is against the 32-bit message and not the 16-bit one, and that is
# not a coincidence. Defects 1 and 2 of that test separate the two.
#
# Two things the advisory says that a version number does not
# -----------------------------------------------------------
# NO CVE WAS ISSUED. It scores 6.9 under CVSS 4.0 — higher than DMTF-2026-0003,
# which does have one. A CVE is a decision about deployment, not a threshold on
# a score.
#
# NOT IN GITHUB'S GLOBAL ADVISORY DATABASE. This is a repository advisory on
# DMTF/libspdm. https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-m4wc-xmvg-369f — the URL a
# reader tries first — returns 404. url= above is the one that resolves.
#
# Released alongside its fix: libspdm 3.8.2 was published at 19:25:51Z on the
# same day, one hundred and thirty-three seconds after this advisory.
#
# Not vendored: the advisory is not this repository's to redistribute. sha256=
# is the digest of the JSON record for this GHSA id as returned by api= on
# retrieved-at, extracted with `jq '.[] | select(.ghsa_id=="...")'`.
# harness/check_advisories.py --refresh re-fetches and reports any drift; the
# weekly `upstream` CI job runs it, so a changed advisory is noticed without
# making every pull request depend on somebody else's API.
